F437|THE SINK IDLE-FILL HAS NEVER RUN AND CANNOT BE REACHED FROM CONFIG|The research F436 asked for, done. gates.sink_review is False in ALL SIX archived cells and the sink_review event was never once emitted - proven with a POSITIVE CONTROL rather than observed, because a bare zero licenses nothing: the sibling gates in the same block (review, smoke) are present and True, so the query can see the field. CAUSE: sink_review_enabled() is ENV-ONLY with unwrap_or(false) in scheduler.rs 47. It is not a SwarmConfig field, so the golden-formula bake could not include it, and the DESKTOP PATH CANNOT REACH IT AT ALL - LaunchServices hands the app its own environment and discards every var, which is the documented reason levers must ride config.yaml. So the one mechanism built to fill the biggest idle window in the run is unreachable in the shipping product. ⚠️ I DID NOT FLIP THE DEFAULT. The engine already documents this history and a prior session ruled that the default stays OFF and that turning it on is an ARM, not a silent flip. That call is correct and I respected it - a silent flip would move the baseline underneath a running campaign. INSTEAD, PROMOTED the existing arm from position 10 of 15, about two days of fleet time away, to CELL 1 OF 19 in c7674275e, and repointed the readout from "does it fire" to the number that matters: the share of the dispatch window at 2 or fewer concurrent must FALL against the 3-node baseline band of 27.6 to 53.9 percent. ⚠️ THE LIKELY ANSWER IS ALREADY IN HAND AND IS REGISTERED AS THE FALSIFIER: the EXISTING pre-review fill uses only 8.6 and 18.9 percent of the sink's idle node-time in the two cells where secs is present, because its work-list is ONE REVIEW PER TASK and exhausts long before the sink does. If sink_review fires with prewarmed above zero and the concurrency share does NOT move, the ceiling is the WORK-LIST and not the lever, and that is a different fix entirely. ⚠️ ORDERING VERIFIED BY RUNNING cells(), not by reading the slice. Takes effect at the next sweep RESTART because a running interpreter holds the old QUESTIONS
F438|THE 3-NODE SWARM IS PENALISED BY A METRIC THAT GETS HARDER PURELY BECAUSE IT HAS MORE NODES|The other half of F436, and the best explanation of the node curve this campaign has produced. THE DETAIL FAN SCALES BEAUTIFULLY: 1334s for 9 details on 1 node (148s each) against 249s for 19, 293s for 17, 588s for 8 and 571s for 21 on 3 nodes (13 to 74s each) - a 2x to 11x speedup, the fan working exactly as designed. THAT GAIN IS THEN HANDED BACK. Every confidence_retarget in every cell carries binding_signal agreement, and the tax is 821s on n3-r0, 786s on n3-r1 and 1657s on n3-r3, which is 40 to 57 percent of the 3-node prefix. THE ONE-NODE CELL PAID ZERO. WHY, and it is structural, not luck: skeleton_drafts shows the 1-node cell REQUESTED 2 drafts (worker_count 2) while the 3-node cells requested 3. Agreement then came out 88 on 2 drafts versus 50, 52 and 54 on 3. plan_agreement scores count-spread as max minus min and file overlap as MEAN PAIRWISE JACCARD, AND THE ENGINE'S OWN DOC SAYS BOTH ONLY WORSEN OR HOLD AS THE POOL GROWS. So a 3-draft set is scored lower than a 2-draft set of equal quality, ask_floor is a FIXED 85, and the run below it pays a full redraft ladder. MORE NODES MEANS MORE DRAFTS MEANS LOWER MEASURED AGREEMENT MEANS A TAX THAT CANCELS THE FAN. TWO FIXES ARE ALREADY BUILT AND BOTH ARE INERT ON THE ROUND THAT DECIDES: diverse_plan resolves FALSE in the runs, and its own comment calls the metric "the bug that makes the SEQUENTIAL retarget ladder run every time" and says ENFORCE lifts a strongly-converging draft set past the floor so the ladder is SKIPPED. best_subset_agreement exists to make a GROWING pool raise rather than lower the score, but round 1 uses it only when consensus_k is set and otherwise calls plan_agreement, and round 1 is exactly the round that decides whether to retarget at all. ⚠️ NOT YET A CLAIM THAT REMOVING THE TAX HELPS: retargeting cells scored 0.9343, 0.7147 and 0.8157 against 0.6030 and 0.6695 for the two that never retargeted, so on this evidence THE TAX MAY BE BUYING THE QUALITY. Three against two, far inside the 0.325 spread. The right experiment is diverse_plan ENFORCE, which removes the tax WITHOUT removing the redraft when drafts genuinely disagree - not retarget_off, which removes both and cannot separate them
F439|68 ORPHANED PROCESSES OVER FOUR DAYS, HOLDING PORTS THE REAPER WAS NOT WATCHING|Found while checking for a duplicate sweep before planning the rebuild. reap_stray_listeners runs between EVERY unit and had found none of them, correctly: it sweeps PORT_BASE to PORT_BASE plus 40, that is 8930 to 8970, calibrated on the single leak ever observed at port 8931. The apps under test pick their OWN ports. The live leaks held 18082, 19000, 18080, 18095 to 18101, 18765, 18123, 19001, 19004 to 19006, 8000, 8081, 8099, 9000, 9090, 9999 and 38081 - NOT ONE inside the range. The instrument was calibrated on one sample and has been finding nothing, correctly, forever. THIS IS NOT COSMETIC: F88 already recorded a stray port failing a whole unit with OSError Errno 48 Address already in use, and a test that imports the app while another process holds its port errors at COLLECT time, so a leaked server presents as a BUILD DEFECT and lands in the score. Several cells scored 0.00 on server_runs. WIDENING THE PORT RANGE IS THE WRONG FIX - 8000, 9000 and 9999 are exactly where a person's own dev server lives and this runs unattended on Mihai's machine - so the discriminator is the CWD, because every one of these was spawned by a worker inside a run directory and nothing of his is in there. AGE IS THE SECOND GUARD AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT BELT AND BRACES: run directories are REUSED, and four processes with cwd swarm-3node-r1 were 2 days 20 hours old while that cell had been running for 80 minutes. Same path, different run. ⚠️ MY FIRST GUARD WAS A COUNTDOWN, NOT A GUARD: a process-GROUP check leaves the live engine matching every condition - goose swarm run is spawned in its own group and its cwd IS a run directory - with nothing but the 3 hour age floor between it and SIGKILL, against cells that take 2.5 hours. Fixed to walk ppid to the root, and protect_root is a PARAMETER because a guard that can only be exercised by BEING the sweep is a guard nobody has ever checked. EXECUTED once by hand at 14:30 because the running interpreter holds the old module: 68 killed, exactly one process left under runs (the live engine), sweep and engine both alive, every leaked port released. ⇒ L246 AN INSTRUMENT CALIBRATED ON ONE OBSERVED SAMPLE WILL KEEP REPORTING CLEAN FOREVER
F440|F432 DOUBLE-CONFIRMED BY A MECHANISM THAT DID NOT FIRE, AND THE RAISED BUDGET IS SPIRAL-SAFE|Checking my own f37b92bf5 for a side effect I might have introduced: raising complete_cap_secs 1200 to 3000 lets the repair loop make TWO fix attempts, so a SPIRALLING fix worker would now burn 2400s of fleet time instead of 1200. That would be a real regression and it is the kind I am obliged to hunt in my own changes. IT DOES NOT HAPPEN. The progress watchdog lives inside the dispatcher's streaming loop and is gated ONLY on activity_key not being integrate-verify. smoke_fix_dispatcher is dispatcher.clone(), the SAME object workers use, and the fix task dispatches as task_id complete-fix - confirmed on disk, .swarm/activity/complete-fix.json exists, so activity_key is Some(complete-fix) and the 900s thinking-only budget APPLIES. A fix worker that streams reasoning without a productive event is cut at 900s, well inside the 1200s hard cap, so two attempts cannot cost more than two productive attempts. AND THE SAME FACT CORROBORATES F432 FROM A SECOND INDEPENDENT DIRECTION. All four archived attempts ran to secs 1200 exactly with agent_ok false, meaning the OUTER tokio timeout fired and the 900s progress watchdog DID NOT. A watchdog that only elapses on continuous thinking-only time can only fail to fire if the worker kept emitting PRODUCTIVE events - real tool calls, results or non-thinking text - for the whole 20 minutes. That is exactly what the activity trace showed independently (11 tool calls, a correct MockStore MagicMock diagnosis, an edit that LANDED and changed round 1's pytest error). So the claim that these workers were WORKING rather than spiralling now rests on TWO unrelated engine mechanisms agreeing, not on one trace I read. ⇒ L247 A WATCHDOG THAT DID NOT FIRE IS EVIDENCE, AND IT IS THE CHEAPEST KIND - IT COSTS NOTHING TO CHECK AND IT IS INDEPENDENT OF WHATEVER YOU ALREADY BELIEVE
F441|THE SINK IS TRUNCATED IN 2 OF 5 CELLS, AND MY EXPLANATION FOR IT DIED ON THE DATA|Extending F436. The sink is not merely the longest phase - IT DOES NOT FINISH. Measured sink duration against sink_capped across every archived cell: baseline-n3-r0 ran 3371s which is EXACTLY its cap (cap_secs 3371, base 1800, tree_bytes 56188) and baseline-n3-r1 ran 2700s which is EXACTLY its cap (base 1800, tree_bytes 45001). Both were CUT OFF, and the engine's own detail says such a sink is finalized as done so the run can terminate - so A TRUNCATED JOIN IS REPORTED AS A COMPLETED JOIN. The other three finished under cap at 1656s, 499s and 1157s. Note the best cell ever recorded, 0.9343, is one of the TRUNCATED ones, so truncation is not obviously harmful and I am not claiming it is. THE HYPOTHESIS I FORMED AND KILLED: bigger fleet builds a bigger tree, bigger tree needs a longer join, so 3-node runs hit the cap and 1-node runs do not - another mechanism where more nodes costs you. FALSE. baseline-n3-r2 has the LARGEST built tree of any cell, 92400 bytes across 12 py files on disk, and finished its sink in 1656s WITHOUT capping, while n3-r0 at 89538 bytes needed more than 3371s. Tree size did not predict duration. That makes TEN hypotheses dead on measurement today, and this one died before it was written up rather than after. ⚠️ THE COMPARISON WAS ONLY POSSIBLE FROM OUTSIDE THE ENGINE, which is the real defect: tree_bytes, cap_secs and cap_base_secs ride sink_capped, an event that fires ONLY when the budget is EXCEEDED. Every sink that finished comfortably reports nothing, so scaled_sink_cap - the formula that decides this budget - CANNOT BE AUDITED against the runs where it worked, and I had to count files with find, which measures a different thing than the engine's frozen dispatch-time manifest. Shipped sink_plan, emitted for EVERY sink with cap_secs, cap_base_secs, tree_bytes and scale, so the ratio can be regressed against actual duration instead of guessed. ⇒ L248 A BUDGET REPORTED ONLY WHEN IT IS EXCEEDED CANNOT BE TUNED, BECAUSE EVERY SUCCESSFUL CASE IS INVISIBLE
F442|ROOT CAUSE OF F438 FOUND IN THE CODE - THE DRAFT COUNT IS SIZED FROM THE FLEET AND THE FIX IS APPLIED ONLY AFTER THE DAMAGE|best_of_n is computed as base.max(devices.len().clamp(1,5)), so a 1-node fleet drafts 2 skeletons and a 3-node fleet drafts 3. The comment there justifies it as free - drafts run in parallel so using all nodes adds no wall-clock and yields a better best-of-N skeleton - and it was written to fix a real complaint about a third node idling during the draft step. IT IS NOT FREE. plan_agreement scores count-spread as max minus min and file overlap as MEAN PAIRWISE JACCARD, and best_subset_agreement's own doc says both only worsen or hold as the pool grows. So the extra draft the fleet buys is scored against it: 1 node 2 drafts agreement 88 and ZERO ladder, 3 nodes 3 drafts agreement 50, 52 and 54 and ladders of 786, 821 and 1657 seconds - 40 to 57 percent of the planning prefix, which is about the whole gain the detail fan just delivered. A FLEET-RELATIVE QUANTITY FEEDS A FLEET-INVARIANT THRESHOLD, ask_floor 85, THROUGH A METRIC THAT DEGRADES WITH POOL SIZE. AND THE FIX ALREADY EXISTS IN THE FILE, POINTED THE WRONG WAY: best_subset_agreement was written so that GROWING the pool can only RAISE the reported agreement, and it is wired in as consensus_k - but its own doc says RETARGET ONLY. So the pool-size-invariant measure is applied only AFTER the ladder has been triggered, never at the round-1 decision point where the pool-size penalty is what triggers it. That is exactly backwards. SHIPPED THE COUNTERFACTUAL, NOT THE BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: plan_convergence now also carries agreement_best2 and pool_penalty, where k equals 2 because that is what a 1-node fleet drafts, so every fleet is reported on the SAME footing and the field is an exact no-op at 2 drafts. A run whose agreement_best2 clears ask_floor while agreement_conf does not is a run whose ladder was bought ENTIRELY by having more nodes, and that is now readable from any ordinary run. ⚠️ DELIBERATELY NOT FLIPPED: the cells that laddered scored 0.9343, 0.7147 and 0.8157 against 0.6030 and 0.6695 for the two that did not, so the ladder may be buying the quality and a silent flip could spend it. ⇒ L249 WHEN A QUANTITY SCALES WITH THE FLEET, EVERY THRESHOLD IT FEEDS MUST SCALE TOO, OR THE BIGGER FLEET PAYS A TAX FOR BEING BIGGER
F443|F433 LANDED IN A DEAD ZONE I LEFT IN MY OWN REGISTRATION, AND F432 IS NOW 5 OF 5|The last cell on the pre-fix binary scored 0.7186 with tier A at a perfect 1.00 and tier B at 0.3611, the sync family again. FOUR READOUTS. (1) F433 JUDGE SKIP-RATE: 60 skipped of 142 opportunities = 42.3 percent. I predicted at or below 40 and set the falsifier at or above 45. 42.3 IS NEITHER. The prediction FAILED and the falsifier DID NOT FIRE, because I left a five-point gap between them and the result landed in it. That is a defect in the REGISTRATION, not a result: a prediction whose failure region does not abut its falsifier can produce an outcome that decides nothing, and I am recording it as INCONCLUSIVE rather than rounding 42.3 down to a pass. Every future registration states one threshold, not two with a gap. (2) F432 IS NOW 5 OF 5: complete_fix_completed at secs 1200, agent_ok false, again. Five archived attempts, zero completed repairs, on the binary that predates the fix. (3) F441 SINK TABLE now n=6 with THREE capped: this cell ran 2574s to exactly its cap on a tree of 42899 bytes, the SMALLEST of the three capped, which is a third independent contradiction of tree-size-predicts-duration. (4) F438 LADDER: this 3-node cell retargeted ZERO times, so the ladder is not universal at 3 nodes - it is a draft-outcome property, which is exactly what F442's mechanism predicts, since a 3-draft pool sometimes still clears the floor. ⚠️ THE REBUILD IS DONE. Eleven commits are now live: engine_build moved from 1785993228-235858864 to 1786021467-235858768, the sweep was stopped at a real unit boundary one minute into a fresh unit that had produced nothing, the run tree was parked with 6 of 6 logs preserved, and the sweep restarted on the new binary with the promoted arm order. ⚠️ ONE VERIFICATION IS OUTSTANDING AND I WILL NOT CLAIM IT: a byte-search for desc_sha in the new binary returns zero by two methods while the POSITIVE CONTROL passes - retarget_discarded, sink_capped, tree_bytes and cap_base_secs are all found, and the source contains desc_sha five times. So the probe is inconclusive for that one key rather than evidence of absence, and the honest test is the first plan_loaded the new binary emits. CHECK IT ON THE FIRST CELL
